(
    title: "Learning to multiply",
    intro: "The method of the 珠心算 calculators: an empty abacus, digits swept left to right and every partial product recorded with two digits. The procedure comes first; speed follows practice.",
    lessons: [
        (
            id: "M0",
            title: "The 大九九 table and two-digit recording",
            goal: "Know the 81 single-digit products and always write them with two digits.",
            paragraphs: [
                "Everything starts with the table: every pair of digits from 1 to 9 has a product worth knowing by heart.",
                "In empty-abacus multiplication every product is recorded with two digits, even when it starts with zero: 2×4 = 08, 3×3 = 09, 7×8 = 56.",
                "That zero is not decorative: it occupies the tens rod and prevents every later digit from shifting.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 2,
                multiplier: 4,
                expected: 8,
                caption: "2 × 4 = 08",
                conclusion: "The product is 8, but it is recorded as 08: the 0 occupies the tens rod and the 8 the units rod.",
            ),
            foundation: "The product of two digits is repeated addition that the 大九九 (9×9) table automates. Two-digit recording is a positional convention: each product occupies exactly two consecutive rods, even when its tens digit is zero.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō / 定位法: for an m-digit factor and an n-digit factor, m + n rods are prepared; if the highest digit of the result lands on an empty rod, apply 前空减一, 'add the digit counts and subtract one'. Here m = n = 1, so two rods are used.",
            procedure: [
                "Pick the digit pair in the 大九九 table and recall the product without counting.",
                "Always write two digits: if the product is below 10, prepend a zero.",
                "Place the tens digit on the left rod and the units digit on the right rod.",
                "Check that the tens rod exists even when its bead is at zero.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Forgetting the leading zero in 01..09: it shifts every following position.",
                "Confusing the small 5×5 table with the 大九九 (9×9), which includes facts such as 7×8 = 56.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "2×4: the table gives 08, not 8.",
                "Rod 1 (tens): 0. Rod 0 (units): 8.",
                "Final reading: 08 = 8, with the position already reserved for a larger calculation.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M1",
            title: "One digit by one digit",
            goal: "Solve one digit by one digit, recording the product on two rods.",
            paragraphs: [
                "The abacus starts empty. The rod on the right is units; the next one is tens.",
                "Multiply the two digits and record the product with two digits: tens on the left, units on the right.",
                "For 7×8 the product 56 is recorded as is. A single-digit product would be completed with a leading zero.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 7,
                multiplier: 8,
                expected: 56,
                caption: "7 × 8 = 56",
                conclusion: "56 occupies two rods: 5 in tens and 6 in units. Nothing else needs to be added.",
            ),
            foundation: "There is no previous total because the abacus is empty (空盘). The product is written directly and its place value comes from the position of the two rods.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: m + n = 1 + 1 = 2 rods. A two-digit product starts on the tens rod; if the first digit is zero the empty rod is kept (前空减一 only applies to the final result).",
            procedure: [
                "Leave the abacus at zero and place the units rod on the right.",
                "Recall the product of the two digits from the 大九九 table.",
                "Record the tens on the left rod and the units on the right rod.",
                "Read the abacus left to right without moving beads already set.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Recording 56 as 5 in units and 6 in tens (reversed order).",
                "Moving the unit point between lessons: the rightmost rod is always units.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "7×8 = 56 from the table.",
                "Tens: 5. Units: 6.",
                "The empty abacus now reads 56; there are no further partial products.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M2",
            title: "Two digits by one digit",
            goal: "Multiply a two-digit number by one digit, left to right.",
            paragraphs: [
                "The one-digit multiplier is held in mind; the multiplicand is swept from left to right.",
                "Each product is recorded with two digits and added to what is already on the abacus. Nothing is cleared.",
                "For 28×3, compute 3×2 = 06 first and then 3×8 = 24. The 24 starts on the units rod of the 06 and advances one more rod to the right.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 28,
                multiplier: 3,
                expected: 84,
                caption: "28 × 3 = 84",
                conclusion: "06 and 24 overlap on one rod: the result is 84.",
            ),
            foundation: "Distributivity: 3×(20+8) = 3×20 + 3×8. Each partial product shifts one rod to the right because its multiplicand digit is worth ten times less than the previous one.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: m + n = 2 + 1 = 3 rods. The first product 06 starts on rods 2 and 1; the next, 24, starts one rod further right (1 and 0) and adds onto the existing beads.",
            procedure: [
                "Read the multiplicand left to right: first 2, then 8.",
                "Record 3×2 = 06 on the two leftmost rods.",
                "Record 3×8 = 24 shifted one rod to the right, adding without clearing.",
                "If a rod exceeds 9, carry the tens to the rod on its left.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Forgetting that 3×2 is 06 and not 6: without the zero, the 24 is misplaced.",
                "Clearing the first product before adding the second.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "Step 1: 3×2 = 06 → tens 0, units 6.",
                "Step 2: 3×8 = 24 → placed one rod to the right; 6+4 = 10.",
                "The carry leaves 84: 0+2 and the 1 from the 10 merge into the higher rod.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M3",
            title: "One digit by two digits",
            goal: "Multiply one digit by a two-digit number by sweeping the multiplier.",
            paragraphs: [
                "The multiplier now has two digits and sets the order of the steps: first its tens, then its units.",
                "Each digit of the multiplier sweeps the whole multiplicand. Here the multiplicand is a single digit, so there is one product per multiplier digit.",
                "It is the same mechanism as M2 with the roles swapped. The final product does not change, but the order of the steps does.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 7,
                multiplier: 34,
                expected: 238,
                caption: "7 × 34 = 238",
                conclusion: "3×7 = 21 and 4×7 = 28; the second product starts on the units rod of the first.",
            ),
            foundation: "The result is commutative but the algorithm is not: 34×7 = 7×30 + 7×4. The multiplier sets the sweep order because its digits determine each shift.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: m + n = 1 + 2 = 3 rods. The pass for 3 starts on rods 2 and 1; the pass for 4 on rods 1 and 0; the result 238 occupies three rods.",
            procedure: [
                "Point at the tens digit of the multiplier (3) and compute 3×7 = 21.",
                "Place 21 on the two leftmost rods.",
                "Point at the units digit (4), compute 4×7 = 28 and place it shifted one rod right.",
                "Add the overlaps and read 238.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Starting with the units digit of the multiplier: the pass order changes the shift.",
                "Assuming 3×7 = 21 should be written as 021: it is two digits, not three.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "Pass for 3: 21 on rods 2–1.",
                "Pass for 4: 28 on rods 1–0; 1+2 = 3 on the shared rod.",
                "Result: 2 · 3 · 8 = 238.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M4",
            title: "Two digits by two digits",
            goal: "Solve 23×45 with four partial products and positional addition.",
            paragraphs: [
                "The multiplier 45 has two digits: first use the 4 against the whole 23, then the 5 against the whole 23.",
                "4×2 = 08 occupies the hundreds and thousands rods; 4×3 = 12 starts one rod to the right. Then come 5×2 = 10 and 5×3 = 15.",
                "Each product is added without clearing the previous one. The result is read when the last pass finishes.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 23,
                multiplier: 45,
                expected: 1035,
                caption: "23 × 45 = 1035",
                conclusion: "08, 12, 10 and 15 placed by place value give 1035.",
            ),
            foundation: "Double distributivity: (20+3)×(40+5) = 20×40 + 20×5 + 3×40 + 3×5. The four products accumulate on their corresponding rods.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: m + n = 2 + 2 = 4 rods. Each multiplier pass starts one rod to the right of the previous one; within a pass, each multiplicand digit advances another rod.",
            procedure: [
                "Sweep 23 with 4: record 08, then 12 shifted one rod right.",
                "Sweep 23 with 5: record 10, then 15 one rod further right.",
                "Add the four contributions on each rod, carrying excesses to the left.",
                "Read the result left to right: 1035.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Crossed carries: a rod above 9 must carry to its neighbour before continuing.",
                "Forgetting the intermediate zeros of 08 and 10, which leave empty rods with place value.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "4×2 = 08 and 4×3 = 12: first pass.",
                "5×2 = 10 and 5×3 = 15: second pass, shifted one rod.",
                "Overlaps resolved right to left: 10 · 35 = 1035.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M5",
            title: "Three digits by two digits",
            goal: "Multiply 213×32 while controlling carries and positions.",
            paragraphs: [
                "The multiplicand now has three digits. Each digit of the multiplier sweeps the whole 213 from left to right.",
                "Within a pass, each product is placed one rod to the right of the previous one.",
                "When the multiplier digit changes, the next pass starts one rod to the right of where the previous pass started.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 213,
                multiplier: 32,
                expected: 6816,
                caption: "213 × 32 = 6816",
                conclusion: "The six partial sums accumulate without clearing anything: 6816.",
            ),
            foundation: "The M4 algorithm with one more digit: 213×32 = 213×30 + 213×2. Each pass has three two-digit products, six in total.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: m + n = 3 + 2 = 5 rods, reduced to 4 by 前空减一 if the highest is empty. First pass from rod 4, second from rod 3.",
            procedure: [
                "With 3: 3×2 = 06, 3×1 = 03, 3×3 = 09, each one rod to the right.",
                "With 2: 2×2 = 04, 2×1 = 02, 2×3 = 06, starting one rod further right.",
                "Resolve carries right to left before reading.",
                "Check that the ten-thousands rod is empty and apply 前空减一.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Losing the order within a pass: the 3 of 213 comes after the 1.",
                "Confusing the shift between passes with the shift within a pass.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "First pass: 06 · 03 · 09.",
                "Second pass: 04 · 02 · 06, shifted one rod.",
                "Sum with carries: 6816.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M6",
            title: "Three digits by three digits",
            goal: "Complete the method with 213×324: nine products and six rods.",
            paragraphs: [
                "This is the full case: three digits by three digits, nine partial products and up to six rods.",
                "Zero control is the delicate part: a zero digit produces 00 and does not shift the other positions.",
                "If the result starts on an empty rod it has one digit fewer: 位数相加，前空减一, 'add the digit counts and subtract one when the front is empty'.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 213,
                multiplier: 324,
                expected: 69012,
                caption: "213 × 324 = 69012",
                conclusion: "Nine products, six rods and an intermediate zero: the result is 69012.",
            ),
            foundation: "The generalisation of the algorithm: every multiplier digit sweeps every multiplicand digit, with positional shift per pass and per digit. It is distributivity applied three times.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: m + n = 3 + 3 = 6 rods prepared; 前空减一 gives five digits when the highest rod stays empty. The unit point is fixed before starting.",
            procedure: [
                "Pass for 3: products 3×213 recorded with two digits each.",
                "Pass for 2: same sequence shifted one rod right.",
                "Pass for 4: same sequence shifted another rod right.",
                "Sum and carry right to left; watch the intermediate zero of the result.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "A zero in the multiplicand yields 00: it must be recorded so later passes do not shift.",
                "Carries crossing between passes; always resolve them right to left.",
                "Forgetting 前空减一 when six rods have an empty leading rod.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "Three passes of three products: 06·03·09, 04·02·06, 08·04·12.",
                "Overlaps are summed without clearing and carried leftwards.",
                "Reading with an intermediate zero: 69012.",
            ],
        ),
        (
            id: "M7",
            title: "Mental: the same sequence without an abacus",
            goal: "Reproduce the product sequence with a mental image of the abacus.",
            paragraphs: [
                "Once the procedure is automatic, cover the abacus and repeat the same sequence of products from left to right.",
                "You need not 'see' anything: holding the running total and adding each product in its position is enough.",
                "Accuracy comes first; time drops on its own with practice. The app measures correct answers, it does not promise prodigy speed.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 23,
                multiplier: 45,
                expected: 1035,
                caption: "23 × 45 = 1035, without an abacus",
                conclusion: "Without an abacus: 08, 12, 10 and 15 placed by position; the final total is 1035.",
            ),
            foundation: "Mental Anzan internalises the physical procedure: the sequence and place value remain, not a photographic image of the beads.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō survives as mental structure: m + n imaginary rods and the 前空减一 rule for the final result.",
            procedure: [
                "Imagine the empty rods and fix the unit point.",
                "Recite the products in the same order as on the physical abacus.",
                "Hold the partial total and add each product in its mental position.",
                "Read the final result and verify it on the abacus before moving on.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Calculating horizontally instead of keeping place value: the most common visual block.",
                "Skipping two-digit recording when visualising the products.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "23×45: mental sequence 08 · 12 · 10 · 15.",
                "Total by positions: 10 · 35.",
                "Result: 1035, verified on the abacus afterwards.",
            ],
            show_steps: false,
        ),
        (
            id: "S1",
            title: "Shortcut: teens times teens",
            goal: "Multiply two numbers between 11 and 19 without every partial product.",
            paragraphs: [
                "Condition: both factors are between 11 and 19. Formula: (10+a)(10+b) = 100 + 10(a+b) + ab.",
                "Start from 100, add ten times the sum of the units and add the product of the units.",
                "If the product of the units exceeds 9, carry into the tens: 13×14 = 100 + 70 + 12 = 182.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 12,
                multiplier: 14,
                expected: 168,
                caption: "12 × 14 = 100 + 60 + 8 = 168",
                conclusion: "The shortcut only holds when both factors are between 11 and 19; otherwise use 空盘前乘法.",
            ),
            foundation: "Binomial expansion of (10+a)(10+b). The 100 term is fixed, 10(a+b) occupies the tens and ab the units, with a carry when ab ≥ 10.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: the result has three rods (hundreds, tens, units) because both tens digits are 1. Set 100 on the hundreds rod before adding.",
            procedure: [
                "Check the condition: both factors between 11 and 19.",
                "Add the units a+b and multiply by 10.",
                "Multiply the units a×b and add the three terms.",
                "If a×b has two digits, carry its tens into the previous column.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Applying it to 21×13: the first factor's tens digit is not 1.",
                "Forgetting the carry when the product of the units exceeds 9.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "12×14: a=2, b=4.",
                "100 + 10×6 + 8 = 100 + 60 + 8.",
                "Result 168, with units 8 and tens 6.",
            ],
            show_steps: false,
        ),
        (
            id: "S2",
            title: "Shortcut: same tens, complementary units",
            goal: "Multiply numbers with the same tens digit and units adding to 10.",
            paragraphs: [
                "Condition: same tens digit a and units b, c with b+c = 10.",
                "Formula: a(a+1)·100 + bc. Write the tens times its successor and append the two digits of the units product.",
                "Example: 43×47 → 4×5 = 20 and 3×7 = 21 → 2021.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 43,
                multiplier: 47,
                expected: 2021,
                caption: "4 × 5 = 20 and 3 × 7 = 21 → 2021",
                conclusion: "If the units do not add to 10, the shortcut does not apply.",
            ),
            foundation: "Identity (10a+b)(10a+c) = 100a(a+1) + bc when b+c = 10: the middle term 10a(b+c) becomes 100a.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: the result splits into two two-digit blocks; a(a+1) takes the upper rods and bc the lower rods, always with two digits.",
            procedure: [
                "Verify that the tens digits match and the units add to 10.",
                "Compute a(a+1) as the high part.",
                "Compute b×c with two digits as the low part.",
                "Check the result with the general method if in doubt.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Writing 3×7 = 21 with one digit when a product is below 10.",
                "Using the shortcut with units adding to 9 or 11.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "43×47: a=4, b=3, c=7.",
                "4×5 = 20 (high part); 3×7 = 21 (low part).",
                "Result: 2021.",
            ],
            show_steps: false,
        ),
        (
            id: "S3",
            title: "Shortcut: same units, complementary tens",
            goal: "Multiply numbers with the same units digit and tens adding to 10.",
            paragraphs: [
                "Condition: same units c and tens a, b with a+b = 10.",
                "Formula: (ab + c)·100 + c². The product of the tens plus the unit gives the hundreds; the square of the unit gives the last two digits.",
                "Example: 34×74 → 3×7 + 4 = 25 and 4² = 16 → 2516.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 34,
                multiplier: 74,
                expected: 2516,
                caption: "3 × 7 + 4 = 25 and 4² = 16 → 2516",
                conclusion: "With different units the shortcut does not apply.",
            ),
            foundation: "Identity (10a+c)(10b+c) = 100(ab+c) + c² when a+b = 10. It mirrors the previous shortcut with tens and units swapped.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: two two-digit blocks; ab+c on the upper rods and c² on the lower rods, always with two digits.",
            procedure: [
                "Check that the units match and the tens add to 10.",
                "Compute a×b + c for the high part.",
                "Compute c² with two digits for the low part.",
                "Concatenate both blocks and verify with the general method.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Forgetting to add c to the product of the tens.",
                "Recording c² without a leading zero when c² < 10.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "34×74: a=3, b=7, c=4.",
                "3×7+4 = 25; 4² = 16.",
                "Result: 2516.",
            ],
            show_steps: false,
        ),
        (
            id: "S4",
            title: "Shortcut: complementary-number multiplication",
            goal: "Multiply a factor close to 100 using its complement.",
            paragraphs: [
                "Condition: one factor is close to 100 (or 1000) and its complement is small.",
                "Formula: A×(100−d) = A·100 − A·d. Shift A two places and subtract A times the complement.",
                "Example: 738×96 = 73 800 − 738×4 = 73 800 − 2 952 = 70 848.",
            ],
            example: (
                multiplicand: 738,
                multiplier: 96,
                expected: 70848,
                caption: "738 × 96 = 73 800 − 2 952 = 70 848",
                conclusion: "The shortcut is a subtraction; if the complement is large, the general method is safer.",
            ),
            foundation: "Distributivity over subtraction: A×(B−d) = A·B − A·d. The complement d = 100−B is small, so A·d is a short calculation.",
            positioning: "Teii-hō: shifting A two rods multiplies by 100; align the subtraction by the unit point and expect chained borrowing.",
            procedure: [
                "Compute the complement d of the factor close to 100.",
                "Write A·100 by shifting A two places left.",
                "Compute A·d and subtract it aligned by the unit point.",
                "Resolve borrows right to left and read the result.",
            ],
            pitfalls: [
                "Subtracting A·d misaligned: the error grows by a factor of ten.",
                "Using the shortcut when d is large; the general method is more reliable then.",
            ],
            annotated_example: [
                "738×96: d = 4.",
                "738·100 = 73 800; 738×4 = 2 952.",
                "73 800 − 2 952 = 70 848.",
            ],
            show_steps: false,
        ),
    ],
    fundamentals: (
        title: "Soroban fundamentals",
        intro: "Six chapters to master the abacus before speeding up: anatomy, fingering, complements, carries and the fade into mental calculation.",
        chapters: [
            (
                id: "C1",
                title: "Chapter 1 — Anatomy and reading",
                goal: "Recognise every part of the abacus and read a number without counting beads one by one.",
                sections: [
                    (
                        heading: "The frame and the dividing bar (hari)",
                        diagram: Some("anatomy"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "The soroban is a rectangular frame with vertical rods and a horizontal bar called the hari that separates heavenly from earthly beads.",
                            "Only beads touching the hari count. The others are at rest and are worth zero, even if they look close to the bar.",
                            "Each rod represents a decimal digit. The rightmost rod is units and is marked with the unit point (teii-ten).",
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Lay the frame flat and check that the hari is horizontal.",
                            "Locate the unit point on the rightmost rod.",
                            "Check that every bead is away from the hari: the abacus is at zero.",
                        ],
                    ),
                    (
                        heading: "Go-dama and ichi-dama",
                        diagram: Some("reading"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "Above the hari there is one heavenly bead (go-dama, value 5); below it there are four earthly beads (ichi-dama, value 1 each).",
                            "A rod reads 0 to 9: 0 when nothing touches the hari, 1–4 by earthly beads, 5 when only the heavenly bead is down and 6–9 when earthly beads join it.",
                            "Reading must be instant: recognise the shape of 5 plus the rest instead of counting beads one at a time.",
                        ],
                        tips: [
                            "Do not tilt the abacus: beads must rest against the hari or the frame.",
                            "Drill reading 5, 6, 7, 8 and 9 before any addition exercise.",
                        ],
                    ),
                    (
                        heading: "Teii-ten and clearing (go-hasan)",
                        paragraphs: [
                            "The teii-ten is the unit point: it anchors place value. Without it, a 3 might silently mean 30 or 300.",
                            "Clearing, or go-hasan, moves the heavenly bead away with the index finger and the earthly beads with the thumb, sweeping left to right.",
                            "Practise until resetting the abacus becomes one gesture, without checking each rod.",
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Sweep the heavenly bead of every rod upwards with the index finger.",
                            "Sweep the four earthly beads downwards with the thumb.",
                            "Verify at a glance that the hari is clear along its whole length.",
                        ],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            (
                id: "C2",
                title: "Chapter 2 — Orthodox fingering",
                goal: "Move beads with the smallest gesture and build stable muscle memory.",
                sections: [
                    (
                        heading: "Thumb and index finger, and nothing else",
                        diagram: Some("fingering"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "The thumb raises earthly beads towards the hari; the index finger lowers them and moves the heavenly bead in both directions.",
                            "The other fingers never touch beads: they stabilise the wrist and frame.",
                            "The fingertip only grazes the bead; one smooth continuous motion is faster and more accurate than several short taps.",
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Rest the hand with a loose wrist and curved fingers.",
                            "Raise an earthly bead with the thumb and lower it with the index without moving the rest of the hand.",
                            "Lower the heavenly bead with the index and raise it with the index, from the hari side.",
                        ],
                        tips: [
                            "Using both fingers for a single bead costs speed on neighbouring rods.",
                            "Practise fingering without looking before adding speed.",
                        ],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            (
                id: "C3",
                title: "Chapter 3 — Direct movement and 5-complements",
                goal: "Master the small friends and their inverse moves in subtraction.",
                sections: [
                    (
                        heading: "When the small friend is needed",
                        diagram: Some("complement5"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "Direct movement is enough when the available earthly beads add up without exceeding 4.",
                            "When there are not enough free earthly beads, lower the heavenly bead (value 5) and give back the difference: that is the 5-complement.",
                            "Subtraction reverses the path: raise the heavenly bead and add earthly beads to compensate.",
                        ],
                        formulas: [
                            (label: "+4", equation: "+4 = +5 − 1", note: "Lower the heavenly bead and one earthly bead."),
                            (label: "+3", equation: "+3 = +5 − 2", note: "Lower the heavenly bead and two earthly beads."),
                            (label: "+2", equation: "+2 = +5 − 3", note: "Lower the heavenly bead and three earthly beads."),
                            (label: "+1", equation: "+1 = +5 − 4", note: "Lower the heavenly bead and four earthly beads."),
                            (label: "−4", equation: "−4 = −5 + 1", note: "Raise the heavenly bead and one earthly bead."),
                            (label: "−3", equation: "−3 = −5 + 2", note: "Raise the heavenly bead and two earthly beads."),
                            (label: "−2", equation: "−2 = −5 + 3", note: "Raise the heavenly bead and three earthly beads."),
                            (label: "−1", equation: "−1 = −5 + 4", note: "Raise the heavenly bead and four earthly beads."),
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Look only at the rod involved and count its free earthly beads.",
                            "If they are not enough, apply the small-friend formula from the table.",
                            "Move the heavenly bead first, then compensate with earthly beads.",
                            "Check the new digit at a glance before moving to another rod.",
                        ],
                        tips: [
                            "4+1 needs a 5-complement: the classic mistake is to treat it as direct.",
                            "Do not read the table while executing: memorise it as pairs 1↔4 and 2↔3.",
                        ],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            (
                id: "C4",
                title: "Chapter 4 — 10-complements (large friends)",
                goal: "Resolve overflows of a decade and chains of carries or borrows.",
                sections: [
                    (
                        heading: "When a rod overflows ten",
                        diagram: Some("complement10"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "If a digit plus the addend exceeds 9, remove the complement to 10 on that rod and carry 1 to the rod on the left.",
                            "The 10-complement can itself need a small friend on the same rod; that combination is covered in chapter 5.",
                            "In subtraction, when a digit is insufficient, borrow 1 from the rod on the left and add the complement to 10.",
                        ],
                        formulas: [
                            (label: "+9", equation: "+9 = −1 + 10", note: "Remove 1 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+8", equation: "+8 = −2 + 10", note: "Remove 2 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+7", equation: "+7 = −3 + 10", note: "Remove 3 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+6", equation: "+6 = −4 + 10", note: "Remove 4 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+5", equation: "+5 = −5 + 10", note: "Remove the heavenly bead and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+4", equation: "+4 = −6 + 10", note: "Remove 6 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+3", equation: "+3 = −7 + 10", note: "Remove 7 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+2", equation: "+2 = −8 + 10", note: "Remove 8 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "+1", equation: "+1 = −9 + 10", note: "Remove 9 and carry 1."),
                            (label: "−9", equation: "−9 = +1 − 10", note: "Add 1 and borrow 1."),
                            (label: "−1", equation: "−1 = +9 − 10", note: "Add 9 and borrow 1."),
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Decide first whether the rod overflows; touch nothing until it is clear.",
                            "Apply the large-friend formula on the current rod.",
                            "Carry 1 to the left, or borrow it in a subtraction.",
                            "If the neighbouring rod also overflows, repeat: that is a chain.",
                        ],
                        tips: [
                            "99 + 1: units carry into tens and tens into hundreds; two chained carries.",
                            "1000 − 1: borrow from the thousands rod and propagate the borrow down to the units.",
                        ],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            (
                id: "C5",
                title: "Chapter 5 — Mixed complements (the family)",
                goal: "Combine the small and large friends when no beads are free.",
                sections: [
                    (
                        heading: "The family of seven",
                        diagram: Some("mixed"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "When a rod has neither free earthly beads nor an available heavenly bead, three moves happen at once: remove the 5-complement, move the heavenly bead and carry 1 left.",
                            "It is called a family because +6, +7, +8 and +9 share the structure +k = +(k−5) − 5 + 10.",
                            "The subtraction inverse changes every sign: −k = −(k−5) + 5 − 10, with a borrow to the left.",
                        ],
                        formulas: [
                            (label: "+6", equation: "+6 = +1 − 5 + 10", note: "Raise 1, raise the heavenly bead, carry 1."),
                            (label: "+7", equation: "+7 = +2 − 5 + 10", note: "Raise 2, raise the heavenly bead, carry 1."),
                            (label: "+8", equation: "+8 = +3 − 5 + 10", note: "Raise 3, raise the heavenly bead, carry 1."),
                            (label: "+9", equation: "+9 = +4 − 5 + 10", note: "Raise 4, raise the heavenly bead, carry 1."),
                            (label: "−6", equation: "−6 = −1 + 5 − 10", note: "Lower 1, lower the heavenly bead, borrow 1."),
                            (label: "−7", equation: "−7 = −2 + 5 − 10", note: "Lower 2, lower the heavenly bead, borrow 1."),
                            (label: "−8", equation: "−8 = −3 + 5 − 10", note: "Lower 3, lower the heavenly bead, borrow 1."),
                            (label: "−9", equation: "−9 = −4 + 5 − 10", note: "Lower 4, lower the heavenly bead, borrow 1."),
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Check that earthly beads are insufficient and the heavenly bead is occupied.",
                            "Apply the small-friend part: +(k−5) or −(k−5).",
                            "Move the heavenly bead in the opposite direction (−5 or +5).",
                            "Close with a carry or borrow on the neighbouring rod.",
                        ],
                        tips: [
                            "The order of the three moves is free, but always use the same order to automate it.",
                            "If the neighbouring rod cannot lend, chain another 10-complement.",
                        ],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
            (
                id: "C6",
                title: "Chapter 6 — From the physical abacus to mental Anzan",
                goal: "Fade the abacus progressively without losing place value.",
                sections: [
                    (
                        heading: "The fading protocol",
                        diagram: Some("fading"),
                        paragraphs: [
                            "The move to mental calculation has five stages: Full (real abacus), Ghost (translucent beads), Rods (rods only), UnitMarks (unit points only) and Blank (nothing).",
                            "Keep the same procedure and rhythm at each stage; only what you see changes. Advance when accuracy stays high across several sessions.",
                            "The mental image must keep the rods and the teii-ten; without that structure numbers 'float' and visual blocks appear.",
                        ],
                        steps: [
                            "Full: solve on the abacus until the procedure is automatic.",
                            "Ghost: beads are faint; you decide their position.",
                            "Rods: imagine the beads resting on the rods.",
                            "UnitMarks: keep only the unit points as anchors.",
                            "Blank: run the sequence with no visual support.",
                        ],
                        tips: [
                            "Do not calculate horizontally: ordinary arithmetic activates a different procedure and breaks the abacus image.",
                            "If a visual block appears, step back one stage instead of forcing it: accuracy returns before speed.",
                            "End sessions when attention drops; Anzan improves with short, correct repetitions.",
                        ],
                    ),
                ],
            ),
        ],
    ),
